WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.194
Welcome to Headstart algebra.

2
00:00:02.214 --> 00:00:04.108
This is Lesson 7, the review.

3
00:00:04.128 --> 00:00:07.211
We'll bring together everything you've learned:

4
00:00:07.231 --> 00:00:11.237
collecting, multiplying, dividing, expanding and factorising.

5
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.011
Here's a summary of the five skills.

6
00:00:15.031 --> 00:00:16.975
Let's go through them one at a time.

7
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.142
To collect like terms, look for the same letters with the same powers,

8
00:00:22.162 --> 00:00:24.237
and add or subtract the coefficients.

9
00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:29.989
To multiply, multiply the coefficients and add the indices of matching letters.

10
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:36.630
To divide, divide the coefficients by their H C F, and subtract the indices.

11
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:37.485
Then cancel.

12
00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:43.344
To expand, multiply every term inside the bracket by the term outside.

13
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:49.278
And to factorise, take out the H C F, and divide every term by it.

14
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.604
Now, some common mistakes to watch for.

15
00:00:54.624 --> 00:00:58.710
First, don't mix up adding and multiplying.

16
00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.052
3x + 4x is 7x, but 3x × 4x is 12x².

17
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.187
Second, unlike terms can't be combined.

18
00:01:06.207 --> 00:01:08.348
2x + 3y cannot be simplified.

19
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:16.353
Third, a minus sign in front of a bracket flips every sign inside.

20
00:01:16.373 --> 00:01:18.303
−(a + 2) is −a − 2.

21
00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:23.980
And fourth, when you factorise, take out the highest common factor,

22
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.847
and divide every term by it.

23
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:28.714
A term that is all H C F leaves a 1 behind.

24
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:32.309
How do you choose the right skill?

25
00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:33.637
Look at the expression.

26
00:01:33.657 --> 00:01:38.313
If the same letters and powers are being added or subtracted, collect like terms.

27
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:42.260
If terms are being multiplied, add the indices.

28
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.412
If terms are being divided, subtract the indices.

29
00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:50.441
If there's a bracket with a term outside, expand.

30
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.830
And if you have a sum with a common factor, factorise.

31
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:04.321
Expanding and factorising are opposites, so you can use one to check the other.

32
00:02:04.341 --> 00:02:08.407
Here, −7p(3q − 4) expands to −21pq + 28p.

33
00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:17.699
And 15a³bc + 9a²c factorises to 3a²c(5ab + 3).

34
00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:21.995
To check a factorised answer, expand it.

35
00:02:22.015 --> 00:02:27.002
3a²c(5ab + 3) takes you straight back to the original expression.

36
00:02:27.022 --> 00:02:31.238
You can also check any answer by substituting a number.

37
00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:34.006
Now it's your turn.

38
00:02:34.026 --> 00:02:36.690
Finish the catch-up tracker in your booklet,

39
00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.801
for any questions you missed in Lessons 4, 5 and 6.

40
00:02:39.821 --> 00:02:43.644
Then do the review questions, and try the quiz on this website.

